Answer: Single inheritance occurs when one class inherits from one parent class. Example: Demo1 ↑ Demo2 If: Demo1 → A, B Demo2 → C, D then Demo2 can access: A B C D Java implementation:

```java
class Demo1 {
int A;
int B;
}
class Demo2 extends Demo1 {
int C;
int D;
}
```

Answer: Multilevel inheritance occurs when inheritance exists through multiple levels. Example: Demo1 ↑ Demo2 ↑ Demo3 The relationship is:

```java
class Demo2 extends Demo1 {
}
class Demo3 extends Demo2 {
}
```

Suppose: Demo1 → A, B Demo2 → C, D Demo3 → E, F A Demo3 object can access: A B C D E F because the properties are available through the inheritance chain.

Answer: Hierarchical inheritance occurs when multiple child classes inherit from the same parent class. Example: Demo1 / \ ↓ ↓ Demo2 Demo3 The Java structure is:

```java
class Demo2 extends Demo1 {
}
class Demo3 extends Demo1 {
}
```

Suppose: Demo1 → A, B Demo2 → C, D Demo3 → E, F Both Demo2 and Demo3 inherit A and B from Demo1.

